            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/39/90939/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                "username": "jaehyoo"
            },
            "createdOn": 1780543454,
            "author": {
                "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                "username": "jaehyoo"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "ikvm_server.cpp",
                    "line": 75,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Min Wang",
                        "email": "wangmin@phytium.com.cn",
                        "username": "wangmin239"
                    },
                    "message": "I suggest substituting lines 75\u201385 with doResize(). It leverages real pixel format and geometry rather than default values, and client iteration overhead inside the function is negligible given zero connected clients at server initialization. Please point out any mistakes in my proposal, as I haven\u2019t validated it yet.\n\nvideo.probePixelFormat();\ndoResize();\nformatInitialized = true;"
                },
                {
                    "file": "ikvm_server.cpp",
                    "line": 75,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the suggestion. You're right that the format-setup block (lines 75\u201384) is identical to doResize(), so I factored it out into a shared applyPixelFormat() helper that both call.\n\nI didn't substitute the whole doResize() call, though. The premise that there are zero connected clients here isn't quite accurate: initializePixelFormat() runs from the newClient hook, and libvncserver links the connecting client into screen->clientHead before invoking that hook (rfbNewTCPOrUDPClient() adds to clientHead, then calls newClientHook()). So doResize()'s client iterator would actually visit the just-connecting client and set cd->skipFrame = frameRate on it \u2014 delaying the very first frame \u2014 plus call setTranslateFunction() mid-handshake.\n\nExtracting just the shared format setup gives us the de-duplication you're after while keeping that client-iteration side effect out of the first-connect path. Updated the patch accordingly."
                },
                {
                    "file": "ikvm_server.cpp",
                    "line": 75,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Min Wang",
                        "email": "wangmin@phytium.com.cn",
                        "username": "wangmin239"
                    },
                    "message": "Got it, thanks for the detailed explanation."
                },
                {
                    "file": "ikvm_server.cpp",
                    "line": 327,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Hariharan Rangasamy",
                        "email": "hariharanr@ami.com",
                        "username": "hariharanrangasamy"
                    },
                    "message": "can we wrap initializePixelFormat with try/catch?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "ikvm_server.cpp",
                    "line": 327,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Min Wang",
                        "email": "wangmin@phytium.com.cn",
                        "username": "wangmin239"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't think try/catch needs to be added here. Increasing the framebuffer inside initializePixelFormat is the only scenario that could potentially throw exceptions, and such operations provide strong exception safety guarantee"
                },
                {
                    "file": "ikvm_server.cpp",
                    "line": 327,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Hariharan Rangasamy",
                        "email": "hariharanr@ami.com",
                        "username": "hariharanrangasamy"
                    },
                    "message": "probePixelFormat() can throw (via elog) when the video device open/ioctl fails.\nIf we want the service to stay up and only reject that client, catch the exception in newClient() and return RFB_CLIENT_REFUSE."
                },
                {
                    "file": "ikvm_server.cpp",
                    "line": 327,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jae Hyun Yoo",
                        "email": "jae.yoo@oss.qualcomm.com",
                        "username": "jaehyoo"
                    },
                    "message": "Good catch - this is actually worse than just a rejected client today. newClient() is a C callback invoked by libvncserver, and serverThread has no try/catch, so an elog thrown from probePixelFormat() propagates across the C frames and escapes the thread function -> std::terminate() -> the whole service dies. So one client connecting while the video device is unavailable currently takes down the daemon.\n\nFixed as you suggested: initializePixelFormat() is now wrapped in a try/catch in newClient(), returning RFB_CLIENT_REFUSE on failure. The cleanup falls out correctly - clientGoneHook is set before the throwing block, so on refuse libvncserver calls clientGone(), which decrements numClients back to 0 and frees the ClientData. formatInitialized stays false, so the next client retries the probe (good for transient device errors).\n\nI also fixed a related fd leak: the VIDIOC_G_FMT failure path in probePixelFormat() threw without closing the already-opened fd. Harmless when it crashed, but now that the service survives and clients keep retrying, it would leak an fd per failed attempt - so it closes the fd before the elog."
                }
            ],
